## Mongrel Folk

Mongrel folk are people the slip has changed. A single jump along the [[Phenomena/Infinite Horizon|Infinite Horizon]] costs a traveler a moment of vertigo and nothing more; a body carried across several worlds at once, without protection enough to hold it together, comes out jumbled. A third arm grows at the neck, scales stand in place of skin, one foot grows while the other shrinks, an eye swells to fill a face worn away. The change never reverses, and a mongrel folk cannot have children with anyone who is not one.

Most live below decks on the great striders, born there and unlikely ever to leave. [[Geography/Istria/Iomenei/Undercroft|Iomenei's Undercroft]] pushes those who cannot work into its Mutant Den, and the [[Org/The Harlequins/Providence|Providence]] keeps a population in the halls below its parking garage, working off debts to the [[Org/The Harlequins/index|Harlequins]].

The far-gone among them keep little of human shape. One is drawn out long and furred in blue and red, with a lantern like an anglerfish's grown where its face had been; another is worn away to a single swollen eye; another is covered in porcupine quills rather than fur. A band of them in the Providence's lower decks spoke a chittering dialect of the dead tongue Earthrum, and one wore a horn carved from the same metal as the strider at its neck. Their halls were covered with drawn music and with writing on perfection, apotheosis and deliverance, and stick figures dancing around an orb, and the music answered the notes on the [[Phenomena/Harmony/Voidsong|Voidsong]] sheet [[People/Anzu|Anzu]] carries, in a different notation.
